# What each shading type is, which painter draws it, and where this
# interpreter and the specification differ about it.
#
# PLRM 4.9.3 names seven shading types. They are not alike: three
# describe a field of colour by a function of position, and four hand
# over a packed stream of vertices and let the painter build the shape
# out of them. A program meets those differences directly -- a type that
# answers rangecheck is a type it cannot use, and a type read wrongly is
# a page that is quietly not what it asked for -- so a feature given to
# one of them is a question that has to be put to the rest.
#
# Membership is DERIVED, twice over and in opposite directions. The
# painters are read out of the dispatch table in data/shade.ps, so a type
# newly given a painter shows up here whether or not anyone remembered to
# say so; and every type the table does not name is offered to shfill and
# must be refused, so a type quietly wired up somewhere else fails too.
#
# Each line is
#
#   <type>  <paints|refused>  <painter>  <source>  <function>  <reason>
#
# painter   the procedure the dispatch table sends this type to, held to
#           data/shade.ps. Two painters take two types each: what the
#           pair share is the reading and the subdivision, and what
#           differs is one number in the geometry, which is why they are
#           one procedure and not two.
# source    where the shape comes from. field: a function over an area.
#           axis: two points and a blend along them. circles: two circles
#           and a blend between them. stream: a packed DataSource of
#           vertices or control points, read by the one reader.
# function  whether the type demands a Function entry -- required or
#           optional -- PROBED, not read. A required one absent must
#           raise; an optional one absent must still paint.
# reason    required. What the type is, in the specification's terms.

1  paints  .fnsh      field    required  a colour for every point of a rectangular domain, given as a 2-in n-out function, PLRM Table 4.12
2  paints  .axialsh   axis     required  a blend along the line between two points, optionally extended past either end, PLRM Table 4.13
3  paints  .radialsh  circles  required  a blend between two circles, which is how a sphere or a cone is drawn, PLRM Table 4.14
4  paints  .meshsh    stream   optional  free-form Gouraud triangles, each vertex carrying an edge flag saying how it joins the last, PLRM Table 4.15
5  paints  .meshsh    stream   optional  the same triangles with the vertices in a lattice of stated width, so no flag is carried, PLRM Table 4.16
6  paints  .patchsh   stream   optional  Coons patches, each bounded by four Bezier curves with twelve control points, PLRM Table 4.17
7  paints  .patchsh   stream   optional  tensor-product patches: a type 6 with four more control points in the middle, PLRM 4.9.3

types 7

# The widths the packed reader accepts, and the widths the
# specification allows.
#
# The four stream types share one reader. PLRM allows a coordinate to be
# 1, 2, 4, 8, 12, 16, 24 or 32 bits, a colour component 1, 2, 4, 8, 12 or
# 16, and an edge flag 2, 4 or 8; the fields of one vertex are packed
# against each other with no gaps, and only the end of a vertex -- or of
# a patch -- is padded to a byte. So most of those widths put a field
# across a byte boundary, and the reader counts bits to reach them.
#
# Which widths this interpreter takes is PROBED rather than read, one
# shading per width with its data packed the way that width requires,
# and held to these lines. A width that stops being accepted fails here
# rather than being discovered by a program whose data will not load.
#
#   width <entry> <bits> <takes|refuses>

width BitsPerCoordinate 1   takes
width BitsPerCoordinate 2   takes
width BitsPerCoordinate 4   takes
width BitsPerCoordinate 8   takes
width BitsPerCoordinate 12  takes
width BitsPerCoordinate 16  takes
width BitsPerCoordinate 24  takes
width BitsPerCoordinate 32  takes
width BitsPerComponent  1   takes
width BitsPerComponent  2   takes
width BitsPerComponent  4   takes
width BitsPerComponent  8   takes
width BitsPerComponent  12  takes
width BitsPerComponent  16  takes

width BitsPerFlag       2   takes
width BitsPerFlag       4   takes
width BitsPerFlag       8   takes

widths 17

# The values each painter's edge flag may take.
#
# A flag says which edge of the shape before this one the new shape
# shares. PLRM gives the two stream painters different sets: a triangle
# joins along one of two edges or starts afresh (0, 1, 2, Table 4.15),
# and a patch has a fourth side to join along (0, 1, 2, 3, Table 4.17).
# So this is a difference between two members of one family that the
# specification puts there, and the register carries it rather than
# leaving it to be rediscovered.
#
# A value outside the set names an edge of a shape the data does not
# describe. There is nothing to paint and nothing to guess, so it is
# refused. ADJUDICATED, and the two implementations neither agree with
# each other nor with themselves: one refuses every value outside the
# set in its triangle painter -- which is what this now does, case for
# case -- and masks to the low two bits in its patch painter, so that a
# patch flag of 5 paints there and is refused here. The other draws
# neither the offending shape nor an error, keeping what it had and
# reading no further. Refusing says what happened, and rangecheck is
# one of the two errors PLRM gives shfill.
#
# Masking is deliberately not done. PLRM says only the low two bits of
# the field are used, and that sentence is about a field WIDER than the
# flag -- which is unreachable here while the reader cannot read a flag
# field narrower than eight bits at all. The high bits of an eight-bit
# field are junk no producer can mean, so masking them away would turn a
# malformed stream into a page. It belongs with a reader that counts
# bits, and should arrive with one.
#
#   flagmax <painter> <largest value the flag may take>
#
# The painters are DERIVED: every painter of a type whose source is a
# stream needs a line, and a line for any other painter fails.

flagmax .meshsh   2
flagmax .patchsh  3

flagmaxes 2

# Where this interpreter and the specification differ, and what is to
# become of each difference. A difference the guard can see and this list
# does not name is a failure; so is a line here naming a difference the
# guard can no longer find, because a line that has outlived its defect
# reads exactly like one that still holds.
#
#   <what>  <settled|thorn|heading>  <reason>
#
# settled  the difference is deliberate and is not going to change
# thorn    a known defect or gap, waiting on work
# heading  being changed now

missing-required  settled  PLRM gives shfill the errors rangecheck and undefinedresult, and a shading dictionary missing an entry its type requires raises undefined here instead, because the entry is fetched and the fetch is what fails. ADJUDICATED against both implementations: a type 2 without its Function raises undefined in both of them as well. The letter of the error list is followed by nobody, and changing it would make this the odd one out rather than the conforming one

mesh-coverage  settled  A mesh triangle covers what the same triangle covers when it is filled. It no longer does so by calling fill: a triangle whose colour can be carried to the device is scan-converted once and painted as a colour plane, rather than halved until each piece is flat enough to fill. What keeps the two together is that this painter and the fill hand the same boundary to the SAME SCAN CONVERSION, so the coverage rule is still one rule and not two. The other painters -- a type 1's domain cells, a type 2's bands, a type 3's circles -- still reduce to paths and call fill. Both implementations cover fewer, which means they have a mesh rasteriser of their own and it insets. MEASURED on a right triangle whose third edge is a diagonal: 528 pixels of 1024 here for both the mesh and the fill, this fill agreeing with theirs pixel for pixel, and their mesh painting 496 -- the thirty-two missing are the diagonal, every one of them. PLRM 7.5.1 says a shape is scan-converted by painting any pixel whose region it intersects however small the intersection, and that the area painted is always at least the area of the shape; it also says these details are not a standard part of the language, so neither answer is the specification's. What insetting buys them is a boundary no two triangles claim twice: MEASURED on two triangles sharing an edge, a shared-edge pixel departs from its neighbours by at most 2.2 grey levels of 255 here and 0.5 there, which is not a seam and is not worth a second coverage rule for shapes that already have one. Neither does insetting make a small triangle vanish -- a triangle a twentieth of a pixel across paints one pixel in all three -- so that argument does not decide it either. The probe holds the shading to the fill, so it is the SHARING that is guarded: a painter scan-converting by a rule of its own, rather than the one the fill uses, retires this line. The probe asks it with ONE COLOUR AT EVERY CORNER, because ink counts pixels that are not the colour of the page: a corner at the top of the component range paints pixels the page colour, which are painted and do not count, and the count then answers about colour instead of coverage. That began to bite when the colour across a triangle became exact enough to reach the top of the range

divergences 2
